<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>2.3. Fourier Sine and Cosine series</title>

  <alert>MTH 401</alert>

  <definition xml:id="def-fourier-sine-series">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For <m>0\le x \le L</m>, a <term>Fourier Sine Series</term> has the form
        <md>
          \ds f(x) = \sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \sin\left(\frac{n \pi x}{L}\right)
        </md>
        where <m>A_n</m> are the <term>Fourier Sine Coefficients for <m>f(x)</m></term>, given by
        <md>
          \ds A_n = \frac{2}{L} \int_{0}^L f(x) \sin\left(\frac{n \pi x}{L}\right) ~dx.
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>Let's consider the function <m>f(x) = x^2 - \pi x</m> on the interval
  <m>[0,\pi]</m>.  Suppose that we want to find the Fourier sine series for
  <m>f(x)</m>;  that is, we want to write
  <md>
  f(x) = \sum_{n=1}^\infty A_n\sin(nx).
  </md>
 </p>
      <p>Find the Fourier coefficients <m>A_n</m> (perhaps with technology?) and graph the function <m>f(x)</m>
  and an approximation to your Fourier series over the interval
  <m>[0,\pi]</m>.  Start with a small number of terms, say <m>n=1,\ldots,10</m>,
  and then add more in.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="3in">
    <statement>
      <p>Last time, we looked at another Sturm-Liouville problem:
  <md>
  \begin{aligned}
    X'' \amp  = -k X \\
    X'(0) \amp  = 0 \\
    X'(L) \amp  = 0. \\
  \end{aligned}
  </md>
  We saw that the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions are
  <md>
  \begin{aligned}
    k = 0, ~~~ \amp  X_0 = 1 \\
    k = \left(\frac {n\pi}{L}\right)^2, ~~~ \amp  X_n =
    \cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right) ~~~\mbox{if ~~} n 
    = 1,2,\ldots 
  \end{aligned}
  </md>
  It turns out that these eigenfunctions also satisfy an orthogonality
  relationship (eigenfunctions from a Sturm-Liouville problem always
  will): 
  <md>
  \int_0^L X_n(x)X_m(x)~dx = \left.
    \begin{cases}
      0, \amp  n \neq m \\
      \frac L2 \amp  n = m &gt; 0 \\
      L \amp  n = m = 0
    \end{cases}
  \right.
  </md></p>
      <p>Suppose we want to write a function as a Fourier cosine series:
  <md>
  f(x) = A_0 + 
  \sum_{n=1}^\infty A_n\cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)
  </md>
  Use the fact that <m>\ds X_n = \cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)</m> thus <m>\ds f(x) = A_0X_0 + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n X_n</m> along with orthogonality
  to find an expression for the Fourier
  coefficients <m>A_n</m> (<m>A_0</m> will be a little
  different than <m>A_n</m> with <m>n &gt; 0</m>).</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <definition xml:id="def-fourier-cosine-series">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For <m>0\le x \le L</m>, a <term>Fourier Cosine Series</term> has the form
        <md>
          \ds f(x) = A_0 + \sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \cos\left(\frac{n \pi x}{L}\right)
        </md>
        with <m>A_0</m> and <m>A_n</m> with <m>n>0</m> called the <term>Fourier Cosine Coefficients for <m>f(x)</m></term> given by 
        <md>
          <mrow>
          \ds A_0 \amp = \frac{1}{L} \int_{0}^L f(x) ~dx
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
          \ds A_n \amp = \frac{2}{L} \int_{0}^L f(x) \cos\left(\frac{n \pi x}{L}\right) ~dx.
          </mrow>
        </md>

      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
<p>
    Does this definition match what you found in problem 2?  (If it doesn't, let's talk!)
</p>


  <exercise workspace="3.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>Find the Fourier cosine coefficients for the function <m>f(x) = x</m>
  on the interval <m>[0,10]</m> and graph the function <m>f(x)</m>
  and an approximation to your Fourier series over the interval
  <m>[0,10]</m>.  Start with a small number of terms, say <m>n=1,\ldots,10</m>,
  and then add more in.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="3in">
    <statement>
      <p>Find the Fourier sine series for the constant function <m>f(x) =
  1</m> on the interval <m>[0,\pi]</m> and create a graph of the function and
  an approximation of your series.  Then find the Fourier cosine
  series for the same function over the same interval.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
